    historyContent: {
      origins: "Derived from the traditional game 'Authors', it was adapted and complexified in South Asia during the mid-20th century. The most likely place of origin based on reports is southern Indiaâ€”Tamil Nadu and Kerala.",
      originCountries: [
        'India',
      ],
      timeline: [
        '1940s: Played in Tamil Nadu and Kerala, India, as reported by players whose families played during this era.',
        '1950s: Becomes a staple in elite Indian engineering and medical colleges.',
        '1980s: Standardized rules for 6-player (2 teams of 3) Literature competitive play emerge.',
        "1990s: Spread to North America through Indian students; known as 'Fish', 'Canadian Fish', or 'Russian Fish' in some circles.",
        'Present: Remains a cult favorite among strategy game enthusiasts in India and the UK university circuit.',
      ],
      evolution: "The game evolved to forbid any physical note-taking, making it a pure test of mental 'active memory' and coordination. The name 'Literature' may have been chosen to reflect the relationship to 'Authors'â€”a game with special cards where players collect sets consisting of an author and their works.",
      cultural: "Commonly played during train journeys and late-night study breaks in India; it is seen as a game for 'brainiacs' and strategic thinkers. The no-note-taking rule emphasizes pure memory and mental agility.",
    },
    setupContent: {
      players: '6 or 8 players divided into two teams of equal size. Teams sit alternately, each player between two opponents.',
